1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:00,918
Now the lab.

2
00:00:00,918 --> 00:00:02,449
We use two datasets:

3
00:00:02,449 --> 00:00:19,744
New York City Airbnb twenty nineteen, forty eight thousand eight hundred ninety five listings, and California Housing, twenty thousand six hundred forty districts, where a value limit shows up at five hundred thousand and one.

4
00:00:19,744 --> 00:00:26,784
We analyse six Airbnb columns, and each step runs on all six with one pandas line, no loops.

5
00:00:27,584 --> 00:00:28,931
Steps one and two:

6
00:00:28,931 --> 00:00:31,175
load the data and find causes.

7
00:00:31,175 --> 00:00:44,715
Eleven listings have a price of zero, three have exactly ten thousand, two hundred thirty nine are above a thousand dollars, and fourteen have minimum nights above three sixty five.

8
00:00:44,715 --> 00:00:53,168
A ten thousand dollar private room in Queens is surely a typing mistake, while a Manhattan penthouse may be real.

9
00:00:53,968 --> 00:00:54,767
Step five:

10
00:00:54,767 --> 00:00:57,644
Z-score on all features in one line.

11
00:00:57,644 --> 00:01:06,193
Subtract the mean, divide by the standard deviation, take the absolute value, and count values above three.

12
00:01:06,193 --> 00:01:17,061
Price has three hundred eighty eight, minimum nights three hundred twenty seven, number of reviews twelve hundred twenty one, and so on.

13
00:01:17,061 --> 00:01:21,455
The Z cut-off for price is eight seventy three dollars.

14
00:01:21,455 --> 00:01:27,688
Z-score finds thirty two hundred outliers in total, most in number of reviews.

15
00:01:28,488 --> 00:01:29,211
Step six:

16
00:01:29,211 --> 00:01:30,978
I Q R on all features.

17
00:01:30,978 --> 00:01:38,447
Compute Q one, Q three, the I Q R, and the lower and upper fences, then count values outside.

18
00:01:38,447 --> 00:01:50,414
Price has two thousand nine hundred seventy two outliers, minimum nights six thousand six hundred seven, and host listings seven thousand eighty one.

19
00:01:50,414 --> 00:02:00,936
In total, I Q R finds over twenty four thousand outliers, and thirty eight percent of homes have an outlier in at least one column.

20
00:02:01,736 --> 00:02:02,584
Step seven:

21
00:02:02,584 --> 00:02:10,060
Isolation Forest on all six features, with contamination of two percent and a fixed random state.

22
00:02:10,060 --> 00:02:14,298
It flags nine hundred seventy eight homes, two percent.

23
00:02:14,298 --> 00:02:24,163
Compared with all listings, these have a much higher minimum stay, a median of twenty nine nights, and are open nearly all year.

24
00:02:24,163 --> 00:02:30,560
The strangest homes are company-run flats for long stays, like the Sonder listings.

25
00:02:31,360 --> 00:02:34,984
L O F finds a price that is odd for its area.

26
00:02:34,984 --> 00:02:42,231
We use latitude, longitude and log price, scale them, and run L O F with fifty neighbours.

27
00:02:42,231 --> 00:02:51,491
It finds four hundred eighty nine outliers, and three hundred sixty four of them are not flagged by I Q R on price.

28
00:02:51,491 --> 00:02:59,946
Two hundred twenty eight are cheaper than the median, like a twenty dollar private room in Staten Island.

29
00:02:59,946 --> 00:03:03,328
I Q R can never catch the very cheap ones.

30
00:03:04,128 --> 00:03:05,156
D B S C A N:

31
00:03:05,156 --> 00:03:15,524
log the data, scale it, then cluster with eps zero point eight and minimum samples twenty; noise points are the outliers.

32
00:03:15,524 --> 00:03:18,437
It finds one thousand eighty nine.

33
00:03:18,437 --> 00:03:32,574
Compared across detectors, Isolation Forest and D B S C A N mostly find the same rows as I Q R, while L O F finds about two hundred fifty new ones that I Q R misses.

34
00:03:32,574 --> 00:03:39,600
Models agree with rules on big values, and help most with odd-for-its-group cases.

35
00:03:40,400 --> 00:03:41,174
Step nine:

36
00:03:41,174 --> 00:03:43,341
fix outliers, one line each.

37
00:03:43,341 --> 00:04:02,072
Removal drops price outliers; capping uses clip with the fences; winsorising clips at the first and ninety ninth percentiles; median imputation masks outliers with the median; group median uses each room type's median; and log uses log one p.

38
00:04:02,072 --> 00:04:11,283
The maximum price falls from ten thousand to three thirty four with capping, and to seven ninety nine with winsorising.

39
00:04:11,283 --> 00:04:20,648
Removal loses rows, and removing on all six columns would lose thirty eight percent of the data, so remove on price only.

40
00:04:21,448 --> 00:04:24,863
Replacing outliers using machine learning:

41
00:04:24,863 --> 00:04:36,736
set the outlier prices to missing, then use K N N imputer or a linear regression on location, minimum nights, availability, reviews and room type.

42
00:04:36,736 --> 00:04:43,648
The median gives every home one oh six; K N N and regression give each its own value.

43
00:04:43,648 --> 00:04:49,422
K N N fills have a mean of one eighty eight, regression one sixty four.

44
00:04:49,422 --> 00:04:54,952
Check for silly values, like a regression fill of forty seven cents.

45
00:04:55,752 --> 00:04:56,481
Step ten:

46
00:04:56,481 --> 00:04:59,318
the price numbers before and after.

47
00:04:59,318 --> 00:05:09,125
The median stays about the same, but the mean, standard deviation and skew drop a lot, so the outliers were causing them.

48
00:05:09,125 --> 00:05:17,392
Skew falls from nineteen to about one, and the standard deviation from two forty to under eighty five.

49
00:05:17,392 --> 00:05:21,120
Log one p brings skew to zero point five five.

50
00:05:21,920 --> 00:05:24,906
Skew of every column before and after.

51
00:05:24,906 --> 00:05:30,327
I Q R capping brings every long-tailed column to a skew of about one.

52
00:05:30,327 --> 00:05:40,856
Winsorising leaves host listings skew at seven, because one company with three hundred twenty seven homes is still far above the rest.

53
00:05:41,656 --> 00:05:42,875
And the charts:

54
00:05:42,875 --> 00:05:49,134
box plots of each version on a log axis, and histograms of raw versus capped.

55
00:05:49,134 --> 00:05:56,368
After capping, the long tails are gone and we can finally see the shape of normal prices.

56
00:05:57,168 --> 00:05:58,170
Step eleven:

57
00:05:58,170 --> 00:06:00,843
model accuracy before and after.

58
00:06:00,843 --> 00:06:06,272
Same linear model, same data split, only the outlier fix changes.

59
00:06:06,272 --> 00:06:17,964
R squared is zero point one two on raw price, zero point four four with I Q R capping, and up to zero point four nine with K N N imputation.

60
00:06:17,964 --> 00:06:24,228
The mean absolute error falls from seventy one dollars to about thirty six.

61
00:06:24,228 --> 00:06:30,576
Fixing outliers made the model about four times better and halved the error.

62
00:06:31,376 --> 00:06:32,592
Key takeaways.

63
00:06:32,592 --> 00:06:37,979
An outlier is far from the rest, unusual but not always wrong.

64
00:06:37,979 --> 00:06:38,588
Causes:

65
00:06:38,588 --> 00:06:43,627
mistakes, rare real values, limits, and a different group.

66
00:06:43,627 --> 00:06:44,148
Types:

67
00:06:44,148 --> 00:06:48,058
global, contextual, collective, multivariate.

68
00:06:48,058 --> 00:06:55,357
Z-score works on bell-shaped data but misses outliers in long tails; I Q R is safer.

69
00:06:55,357 --> 00:07:03,351
Ignoring outliers gives a wrong mean and standard deviation, even a flipped regression line.

70
00:07:03,351 --> 00:07:04,480
Fix by cause:

71
00:07:04,480 --> 00:07:11,779
mistakes get fixed or removed, real values get capped or logged, limits get flagged.

72
00:07:11,779 --> 00:07:14,559
Always compare before and after:

73
00:07:14,559 --> 00:07:21,076
skew nineteen to one, R squared zero point one two to zero point four four.

74
00:07:21,076 --> 00:07:32,110
ML models find cases simple rules miss, and K N N and regression give each outlier a smart new value, but check it makes sense.

75
00:07:32,110 --> 00:07:33,848
Thanks for watching.

